---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Your First Five Minutes With Weft"
  description="Install Weft, start its infrastructure, save a memory. Congrats! You're building a second brain."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <h1>Getting Started</h1>
      <p class="stance">
        Five steps and about five minutes is all that's between your agent and a memory that
        persists across conversations, harnesses, and devices.
      </p>
      <div class="callout">
        <strong>Prerequisites.</strong> Python 3.12+ and <a href="https://docs.astral.sh/uv/" rel="noopener">uv</a>.
        Docker Desktop is needed for <code>weft up</code> (local Postgres + Redis) and the
        database-backed test suite. Only Docker is supported at this time.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <div class="step">
        <h2><span class="step-num">01</span>Install Weft</h2>
        <p>Recommended: pipx, for system-wide availability.</p>
        <pre><code>pipx install git+https://github.com/MennoAf/weft.git</code></pre>
        <p>Or as a uv tool:</p>
        <pre><code>uv tool install git+https://github.com/MennoAf/weft.git</code></pre>
        <p>For local development, clone and sync instead:</p>
        <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/MennoAf/weft.git && cd weft && uv sync</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h2><span class="step-num">02</span>Start The Weft Infrastructure</h2>
        <p>
          One command launches Weft's local infrastructure — Postgres 16 with pgvector on port
          5433 and Redis 7 on port 6380 — then runs migrations.
        </p>
        <pre><code>weft up</code></pre>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h2><span class="step-num">03</span>Register Weft As An MCP Server</h2>
        <p>
          Add the Weft MCP server to your agent harness, using the harness's own MCP
          documentation for its configuration format. Then give your agent the copy-paste
          <a href="https://github.com/MennoAf/weft/blob/main/AGENTS.md" rel="noopener">memory protocol</a>
          so it uses Weft tools instead of flat-file memories.
        </p>
        <p>
          Claude-specific setup lives in the repo's
          <a href="https://github.com/MennoAf/weft/blob/main/CLAUDE.md" rel="noopener">CLAUDE.md</a>;
          harness-neutral connection steps are in the
          <a href="https://github.com/MennoAf/weft/blob/main/docs/wiring-your-agent.md" rel="noopener"
            >agent wiring guide</a
          >.
        </p>
        <p>
          You'll also want <code>prime</code> and <code>handoff</code> as skills your agents can
          access — ready-made copies are in
          <a href="https://github.com/MennoAf/weft/tree/main/templates/commands" rel="noopener"
            >templates/commands/</a
          >.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h2><span class="step-num">04</span>Point Your Agent At Weft</h2>
        <p>
          With your Agents.md updated, your agent saves with <code>weft_remember</code>, recalls
          with <code>weft_recall</code>, treating flat files as fallback-only. A quick way to
          verify the instructions stuck:
        </p>
        <div class="callout">
          Ask your agent: <em>“Without me telling you, where do you save memories?”</em><br />
          Expected answer: “Weft, via <code>weft_remember</code>. The flat-file system is
          fallback-only.”
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h2><span class="step-num">05</span>First Session</h2>
        <p>
          Start a session and ask the agent to call
          <code>weft_prime(disclosure="progressive")</code>. If you installed the skills, just use
          <code>/prime</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first time you do this, you'll get a notification that there are no memories yet.
        </p>
        <p>
          Memories are scoped per project, and a project is just a name: pick one name per repo
          and stick to it. Tell your agent to save a memory, for example:
        </p>
        <pre><code>Save: I prefer test descriptions in the form "test_&lt;thing&gt;_&lt;condition&gt;_&lt;outcome&gt;"</code></pre>
        <p>
          Then write the project name into the repo's <code>AGENTS.md</code> (or your harness's
          equivalent instructions file), so every agent that works in this repo — on any machine —
          loads and saves to the same brain. At the end of a non-trivial session, ask it to call
          <code>weft_handoff</code>; a later session's <code>weft_prime</code> will surface both.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="step">
        <h2>Going Deeper</h2>
        <p>
          Every tool and parameter:
          <a href="https://github.com/MennoAf/weft/blob/main/docs/tools.md" rel="noopener">docs/tools.md</a>.
          Every CLI command:
          <a href="https://github.com/MennoAf/weft/blob/main/docs/cli.md" rel="noopener">docs/cli.md</a>.
          Configuration, environment variables, and infrastructure:
          <a href="https://github.com/MennoAf/weft/blob/main/docs/configuration.md" rel="noopener"
            >docs/configuration.md</a
          >.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
